# Type-only modules → TypeScript

Files that **only define types** (no runtime values, no enums, no `Object.freeze` data) belong in **`.ts`**, not `.js` with `@typedef` + `export {}`.

Pair with [`typescript-types-only.mdc`](typescript-types-only.mdc) for what is allowed inside those files.

## Use `.ts` when

- Shared context / DTO shapes with no runtime
- Module augmentations (`declare module '…'`)
- Package-facing `export type` barrels when needed

## Stay in `.js` when

- Enums / constants — see [`js-enum-pattern.mdc`](js-enum-pattern.mdc)
- Runtime logic, CLIs, stages, varlock helpers

## Don't

```js
// BAD — type-only stub in .js
/** @typedef {object} SyncResult */
export {}
```

```ts
// GOOD
export type SyncResult = { synced: number; skipped: number }
```
